        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "File-Based CAS Put Operation\n└── Store.Put() entry \u003c-- 2a\n    ├── Create temp file in cas/tmp/ \u003c-- 2b\n    ├── Stream content loop \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/file_store.go:97\n    │   ├── Read chunks from input \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/file_store.go:101\n    │   ├── Write to SHA256 hasher \u003c-- 2c\n    │   └── Write to temp file \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/file_store.go:106\n    ├── Finalize digest string \u003c-- 2d\n    ├── Check for duplicate object \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/file_store.go:121\n    │   └── handleDuplicateObject() \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/file_store.go:130\n    │       └── merge tags if exists \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/file_store.go:148\n    ├── finalizeUpload() \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/file_store.go:127\n    │   ├── Close temp file \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/file_store.go:167\n    │   ├── Atomic rename to sha256/\u003chex\u003e \u003c-- 2e\n    │   └── writeMetadata() \u003c-- 2f\n    │       ├── Marshal metadata to JSON \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/file_store.go:389\n    │       ├── Write to temp file \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/file_store.go:398\n    │       └── Atomic rename to \u003chex\u003e.json \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/file_store.go:407\n    └── Return Object with digest \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/file_store.go:187",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nThe file-based CAS (Content-Addressable Storage) solves a fundamental problem: **how to store arbitrary content exactly once, with guaranteed integrity, and retrieve it by its cryptographic fingerprint**. Instead of organizing files by name or path, content is stored by its SHA256 hash digest. This means identical content is automatically deduplicated, and any corruption is immediately detectable. The system is used throughout agentctl to store large outputs, artifacts, and cached results without cluttering the filesystem with duplicate data.\n\n## Details\n\n### Storage Layout\n\nThe file store uses a simple directory structure [2a]:\n- `cas/sha256/` - Content files named by their hex digest\n- `cas/sha256/*.json` - Metadata files (size, kind, tags, timestamps)\n- `cas/tmp/` - Temporary files during upload\n- `cas/pins/` - Pin markers to prevent garbage collection\n\n### Put Operation Flow\n\n**Atomic write-then-rename** ensures integrity [2b]. Content is first written to a temporary file in `cas/tmp/` while simultaneously streaming through a SHA256 hasher [2c]. This dual-write pattern computes the digest without requiring the entire content in memory. Once complete, the digest is formatted as `sha256:\u003chex\u003e` [2d].\n\n**Deduplication happens automatically** [2e]. If an object with the same digest already exists, the temp file is discarded and tags are merged. Otherwise, the temp file is atomically renamed to `cas/sha256/\u003chex\u003e` [2e], making it visible to readers only after the write completes successfully.\n\n**Metadata is persisted separately** [2f]. A JSON file at `cas/sha256/\u003chex\u003e.json` stores size, MIME type, tags, and timestamps. This metadata write also uses atomic temp-then-rename to prevent partial writes [2f].\n\n### Key Properties\n\n- **Content-addressed**: Files are retrieved by digest, not path\n- **Atomic writes**: Rename operations ensure readers never see partial content\n- **Automatic deduplication**: Identical content stored only once\n- **Integrity verification**: Digest mismatches detected on read\n- **Tag merging**: Multiple puts of same content accumulate tags"
    },
