        },
        {
          "id": "3e",
          "path": "internal/storage/cas/sqlite_store.go",
          "lineNumber": 129,
          "lineContent": "_, err = s.db.ExecContext(ctx, `\n\t\tINSERT INTO cas_objects (digest, size, kind, tags, pinned, content, created_at, updated_at)\n\t\tVALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, 0, ?, ?, ?)\n\t`, digest, size, kind, tagsJSON, content, timeStr, timeStr)",
          "title": "Insert into database",
          "description": "Store blob content and metadata in cas_objects table"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "SQLite CAS Put Operation\n├── NewSQLiteStore() factory \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/sqlite_store.go:33\n│   ├── dbdriver.OpenDB() \u003c-- 3a\n│   │   ├── Validate config \u003c-- internal/storage/dbdriver/driver.go:92\n│   │   ├── openSQLite() \u003c-- internal/storage/dbdriver/driver.go:98\n│   │   └── migrateSQLiteSchema() \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/sqlite_store.go:60\n│   └── Return SQLiteStore instance \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/sqlite_store.go:53\n└── Put() operation \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/sqlite_store.go:88\n    ├── io.ReadAll(r) \u003c-- 3b\n    ├── sha256.Sum256(content) \u003c-- 3c\n    ├── Check for duplicate \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/sqlite_store.go:107\n    │   └── QueryRowContext(SELECT digest) \u003c-- 3d\n    └── Insert new object \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/sqlite_store.go:128\n        └── ExecContext(INSERT INTO cas_objects) \u003c-- 3e",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nWhen agentctl stores large outputs (like skill results or job outputs), it needs a **content-addressable storage (CAS)** system to avoid bloating JSON envelopes. The SQLite-backed CAS implementation [3a] provides a **database-driven alternative** to the legacy file-based storage, offering better concurrency, atomic operations, and easier querying while storing content blobs directly in the database.\n\nThe core problem: skills and jobs can produce megabytes of output, but the protocol expects compact JSON responses. CAS solves this by storing large content once, referenced by its SHA256 digest, and returning just the digest (or a hint) in the response.\n\n## Details\n\n### Store Initialization\n\nThe `NewSQLiteStore` factory [3a] creates a SQLite database connection using the unified `dbdriver.OpenDB` interface. It configures a **blob threshold** (default 1MB) that determines inline vs external storage, enables WAL mode for concurrency, and runs schema migrations to create the `cas_objects` table with columns for digest, size, kind, tags, pinned status, and the content blob itself.\n\n### Put Operation Flow\n\nWhen storing content [3b], the SQLite implementation **reads the entire input into memory** (unlike the file-based store which streams). This is necessary because SQLite requires the complete blob for insertion. The content is hashed with SHA256 [3c] to generate the digest in format `sha256:\u003chex\u003e`.\n\nBefore inserting, the store **checks for duplicates** [3d] by querying the existing digest. If found, it merges tags and returns the existing object. This deduplication is automatic and ensures identical content is stored only once.\n\nFor new objects, a single SQL INSERT [3e] atomically stores the digest, metadata (size, kind, tags, timestamps), and the content blob. The `pinned` column defaults to 0, allowing garbage collection unless explicitly pinned.\n\n### Key Design Choices\n\n- **In-memory read**: Simplifies implementation but limits to reasonable-sized objects (controlled by blob threshold)\n- **Single table design**: All metadata and content in `cas_objects` for simplicity\n- **Tag merging**: Duplicate puts merge tags rather than error, supporting incremental tagging\n- **Atomic operations**: Database transactions ensure consistency during concurrent access"
    },
