      "traceTextDiagram": "Runservice Output Handling\n├── HandleResult() orchestration \u003c-- internal/runservice/result.go:17\n│   └── enforceOutputLimit() \u003c-- 4a\n│       ├── Check CAS policy enabled \u003c-- 4b\n│       ├── Check size \u003e inline limit \u003c-- internal/runservice/result.go:66\n│       ├── Open CAS store \u003c-- internal/runservice/result.go:70\n│       ├── casStore.Put() stores content \u003c-- 4c\n│       ├── buildOutputWrapperWithPolicy() \u003c-- 4d\n│       │   ├── Extract summary from original \u003c-- internal/runservice/result.go:123\n│       │   ├── Switch on expose policy \u003c-- 4e\n│       │   │   ├── case ExposePolicyOff \u003c-- internal/runservice/result.go:141\n│       │   │   │   └── data[\"stored\"] = true \u003c-- internal/runservice/result.go:144\n│       │   │   ├── case ExposePolicyDigest \u003c-- internal/runservice/result.go:145\n│       │   │   │   └── data[\"artifact\"] = digest \u003c-- internal/runservice/result.go:147\n│       │   │   └── case ExposePolicyHint \u003c-- 4f\n│       │   │       ├── data[\"artifact\"] = digest \u003c-- internal/runservice/result.go:151\n│       │   │       └── Build hint commands \u003c-- 4g\n│       │   └── Return wrapper envelope \u003c-- internal/runservice/result.go:163\n│       └── Marshal and return wrapped output \u003c-- internal/runservice/result.go:93\n└── Write envelope to stdout \u003c-- internal/runservice/result.go:52",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nSkills and jobs in agentctl can produce arbitrarily large outputs—megabytes of JSON, logs, or data. Sending these directly to LLMs or storing them inline creates two problems: **context window exhaustion** and **storage bloat**. The output truncation system solves this by automatically storing large outputs in content-addressable storage (CAS) and replacing them with compact summaries, while **policy settings control whether and how users can retrieve the full content**.\n\n## Details\n\n### Size Enforcement\n\nWhen a job completes, `HandleResult()` [4a] calls `enforceOutputLimit()` to check if the output exceeds the configured inline limit (default 32KB). The system first checks two gates: the `NoCAS` flag and the `CAS.Store` config setting [4b]. If either disables CAS, the full output passes through unchanged.\n\nFor oversized outputs, the system stores the complete content in CAS with the tag `\"truncated-output\"` [4c], generating a content-addressable digest like `sha256:abc123...`.\n\n### Policy-Based Exposure\n\nThe **expose policy** determines what users see in the truncated output [4d]. This is critical for different deployment contexts:\n\n- **`off`**: Stores content for debugging but hides the digest entirely—useful for hooks and internal tools where retrieval isn't needed [4e]\n- **`digest`**: Includes the raw `sha256:...` digest in the output—minimal exposure for programmatic access\n- **`hint`**: Includes the digest plus user-friendly retrieval commands like `agentctl cas read \u003cdigest\u003e` [4f, 4g]—best for interactive skills where users may want to inspect full output\n\nThe policy is configured via `AGENTCTL_CAS_EXPOSE` environment variable or the `cas.expose` config field, with `\"off\"` as the default to prevent accidental information leakage.\n\n### Wrapper Construction\n\n`buildOutputWrapperWithPolicy()` [4d] preserves the original envelope structure (status, command, version) while replacing the data section with a truncation summary. It extracts any existing summary or preview field from the original output and includes metadata like `original_size` and `truncate_reason`. The wrapper format ensures downstream consumers can detect truncation and handle it appropriately."
    },
